steps.body:
A pushbutton is two metal contacts. When they close, they physically bounce for a few milliseconds (typically 1–10 ms) before settling. The Arduino samples `digitalRead` millions of times per second, so it can see HIGH-LOW-HIGH-LOW… several times for one press. A sketch that counts presses on every LOW reading, or even every HIGH→LOW change, will over-count.

Two separate ideas students mix up:
1. **Edge detection:** act on the *change* (released → pressed), not the *level*. Otherwise holding the button triggers your action every loop.
2. **Debouncing:** only accept a change once the reading has been stable for some time (say 30–50 ms).

```arduino
const int BUTTON = 2;
const int LED = 8;
const unsigned long DEBOUNCE_MS = 30;

int stableState = HIGH;          // last accepted state (HIGH = released with pull-up)
int lastReading = HIGH;          // last raw reading
unsigned long lastChange = 0;    // when the raw reading last changed
bool ledOn = false;

void setup() {
  pinMode(BUTTON, INPUT_PULLUP);
  pinMode(LED, OUTPUT);
}

void loop() {
  int reading = digitalRead(BUTTON);
  if (reading != lastReading) {          // raw signal moved: restart the timer
    lastChange = millis();
    lastReading = reading;
  }
  if (millis() - lastChange > DEBOUNCE_MS && reading != stableState) {
    stableState = reading;               // the change has lasted: accept it
    if (stableState == LOW) {            // falling edge = a new press
      ledOn = !ledOn;
      digitalWrite(LED, ledOn);
    }
  }
}
```

`millis() - lastChange > DEBOUNCE_MS` with `unsigned long` stays correct even when `millis()` wraps after about 49.7 days. Never use `delay(50)` to debounce in real projects: it freezes everything else, the same lesson as Week 7's blink-without-delay.

Hardware alternative: a 100 nF capacitor across the button smooths the bounce (an RC filter, Week 4). Good to mention; software is what students will use.

This file won't compile. Why? And what is the smallest change that fixes it without moving `main`?

```cpp-snippet
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::cout << square(4) << '\n';
}

int square(int x) { return x * x; }
```

Second question: if you put `int square(int x) { return x * x; }` in a header and include that header from two different `.cpp` files, what do you predict happens when you link them?

**The compiler reads top to bottom, one file at a time.** When it reaches `square(4)` it must already know that `square` exists, what it takes and what it returns. It does not look ahead, and it never looks into other `.cpp` files. That's the whole reason headers exist.

**Declaration vs definition.**

- A *declaration* introduces a name and its type: `int square(int x);` (note the semicolon, no body). It's a promise: "this exists somewhere."
- A *definition* provides the body: `int square(int x) { return x * x; }`.

You can declare something many times; you may define it only once in the whole program (the *one definition rule*). A forward declaration above `main` fixes the pretest:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

int square(int x);          // declaration: enough for the compiler

int main() {
    std::cout << square(4) << '\n';   // 16
    return 0;
}

int square(int x) {         // definition: needed by the linker
    return x * x;
}
```

**Scaling up: one header per module.** In Python, `import sensors` gives you a module. In C++, you split a module into two files:

- `sensors.h`: the *interface*. Declarations only, plus constants and types callers need.
- `sensors.cpp`: the *implementation*. Definitions. It includes its own header so the compiler checks that declaration and definition agree.
- `main.cpp` includes `sensors.h` and calls the functions.

```cpp-snippet
// sensors.h
#pragma once

namespace sensors {
    constexpr int adc_max = 1023;
    double adc_to_volts(int raw, double vref);
    int volts_to_adc(double volts, double vref);
}
```

```cpp-snippet
// sensors.cpp
#include "sensors.h"
#include <cmath>

namespace sensors {
    double adc_to_volts(int raw, double vref) {
        return raw * vref / adc_max;
    }
    int volts_to_adc(double volts, double vref) {
        return static_cast<int>(std::lround(volts / vref * adc_max));
    }
}
```

```cpp-snippet
// main.cpp
#include <iostream>
#include "sensors.h"

int main() {
    std::cout << sensors::adc_to_volts(512, 5.0) << '\n';   // about 2.502
}
```

Quotes (`"sensors.h"`) search your project folder first; angle brackets (`<iostream>`) search the system include paths.

**Building it.** Either all at once, or one object file per `.cpp` and then link:

```bash
g++ -std=c++20 -Wall -Wextra -c sensors.cpp      # -> sensors.o
g++ -std=c++20 -Wall -Wextra -c main.cpp         # -> main.o
g++ sensors.o main.o -o app                      # link
./app
```

Separate compilation is why big projects build quickly: change `main.cpp` and only `main.o` is rebuilt. (CMake, in Week 14, automates exactly this.)

**Header guards.** `#pragma once` (or the classic `#ifndef SENSORS_H / #define SENSORS_H / #endif`) stops the same header being pasted twice into *one* `.cpp` file, for example when `main.cpp` includes `sensors.h` and also `filters.h`, which itself includes `sensors.h`. Without a guard you'd get "redefinition" errors for the `constexpr` and any types.

**What guards don't fix.** Guards work per `.cpp` file. If a header contains a *function definition* (with a body) and two `.cpp` files include it, each object file gets its own copy, and the linker reports `multiple definition of 'square(int)'`. Fixes: keep only the declaration in the header and the body in one `.cpp`, or mark the function `inline`, which tells the linker that identical copies are fine (that's how header-only libraries work).

**Namespaces** stop names colliding across modules: `sensors::adc_to_volts` and `motors::adc_to_volts` can coexist. Avoid `using namespace std;` in headers: it leaks into every file that includes yours.

**Reading errors fast.** Two error families:

| Message | Stage | Usual cause |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `'square' was not declared in this scope` | compiler | missing `#include` or forward declaration |
| `undefined reference to 'sensors::adc_to_volts(int, double)'` | linker | .cpp not in the build, or declaration and definition signatures differ |
| `multiple definition of ...` | linker | a function body in a header included twice, or the same function defined in two .cpp files |

**Arduino link.** An Arduino sketch hides this: the IDE generates forward declarations for your functions and compiles each `.cpp`/`.h` tab separately. When you write your own Arduino library in Week 14, this is the layout you'll use.

